y )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ |  |\n| ${f}^{″}$ |  |  | $-$ |\n| $f$ |  |  |  |\n\nThis gives us the following information about the graph of $f:$\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ |  |\n| ${f}^{″}$ |  |  | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | Zero slope | $⌢$ |\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x)$ has a zero slope when $x=2,$\n\n- $f(x)$ is decreasing on $(-\\infty ,2),$ and\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each graph:\n\n|  | I | II | III | IV | V |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Zero slope when $x=2$ | $\\times $ | $\\times $ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ |\n| $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ | $✓$ |\n| $⌢$ on $(2,\\infty )$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows: \n\n![](images/24/24cd872d8ab5d9d3e911afad64de02e541e2d877dae1e9f6925a5afa22e1f0fd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f6dbeedf-616d-5b63-a164-72b952d4ada5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507583}}] :activity/title "Sketching a Function Given Some Derivative Properties" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506670}]}
